\title{Embedding}
\description{
A simple lookup table that stores embeddings of a fixed dictionary and size.
This module is often used to store word embeddings and retrieve them using indices.
The input to the module is a list of indices, and the output is the corresponding
word embeddings.
}
\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_embedding]{torch::nn_embedding()}} when trained.

The input is a tensor of indices, e.g. the output of \code{\link{PipeOpTorchIngressCategorical}}, and the
output has one more dimension than the input, namely \code{embedding_dim}.
The indices start at 1, as they do everywhere in \code{torch}'s R interface.

Note that a single table is shared by all positions of the input, so applying this to the
categorical features of a task embeds two features that happen to have the same code into the
same vector. Use \code{\link[=mlr_pipeops_nn_tokenizer_categ]{nn("tokenizer_categ")}} for one table per
feature.
}

\section{Parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{embedding_dim} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The dimension of the embedding.
\item \code{num_embeddings} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
The number of rows of the embedding table, i.e. the largest index that can be looked up.
If this is not set, it is inferred from the task as the largest number of categories over its
categorical features, which is what the categorical ingress produces codes for. It has to be
set for inputs that do not come from a task's categorical features, such as a
\code{\link{lazy_tensor}} of token ids.
\item \code{padding_idx} :: \code{integer(1)}\cr
An index whose embedding is fixed to zero and does not receive gradients. Default is \code{NULL}.
\item \code{max_norm} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
If set, embeddings with a larger norm are renormalized to it. Default is \code{NULL}.
\item \code{norm_type} :: \code{numeric(1)}\cr
The exponent of the norm used for \code{max_norm}. Default is \code{2}.
\item \code{scale_grad_by_freq} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether to scale the gradients by the inverse frequency of the indices in the batch.
Default is \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{sparse} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether the gradient of the embedding table is a sparse tensor. Default is \code{FALSE}.
}
}

